(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-1-pharm-exam-1-master-exam-form-a"]={"title":"Pharmacology I Exam 1 Master Exam — Form A","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"How do macrolides interact with other drugs?","choices":["They induce CYP3A, lowering substrate levels","They chelate divalent cations, reducing absorption of co-administered drugs","They displace substrates from plasma protein binding sites","Metabolites inactivate CYP3A, raising substrate levels"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The ranking is the practical point: azithromycin is the one to reach for when interactions matter. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"How is flucytosine selective for fungal cells?","choices":["It inhibits a fungal cytochrome P450 enzyme with no human counterpart","A fungal transporter pumps it in; human cells lack it","Cytosine deaminase converts it; human cells lack that enzyme","It binds ergosterol, which human membranes do not contain"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The missing human enzyme is the whole basis of selectivity. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"Which vascular beds does dopamine dilate, and through which receptors?","choices":["Renal and splanchnic, through dopaminergic receptors","Coronary and cerebral, through beta-two receptors","Skeletal muscle beds, through alpha-two receptors","Pulmonary vessels, through dopaminergic receptors"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which is distinct from its beta-one cardiac and high-dose alpha-one effects. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"At which enzyme do azoles and allylamines each act?","choices":["Azoles inhibit 1,3-beta-D-glucan synthase; allylamines inhibit lanosterol demethylase","Both inhibit lanosterol demethylase, differing only in binding site","Azoles inhibit lanosterol demethylase; allylamines inhibit squalene epoxidase","Azoles inhibit squalene epoxidase; allylamines inhibit lanosterol demethylase"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Same pathway, different steps — which is why both end in decreased ergosterol. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"What are the two limits on pralidoxime?","choices":["It works only in children, and only within one hour of exposure","It must be given orally, and it is inactivated by food","It reverses muscarinic but not nicotinic signs","It does not enter the brain, and cannot overcome reversible inhibitors"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It reactivates inhibited acetylcholinesterase but only within those limits. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What is propranolol used for beyond hypertension?","choices":["Glaucoma, asthma and benign prostatic hyperplasia","Shock, cardiac arrest and anaphylaxis","Pheochromocytoma and clonidine withdrawal","Migraine, hyperthyroidism, angina and infarction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and after infarction it reduces infarct size and sudden death. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 116"},{"q":"Which benzoyl peroxide effect must be raised before starting it?","choices":["It can bleach hair, clothing and bedding","It causes a permanent blue-grey discolouration of the skin","It commonly causes systemic hypersensitivity reactions","It stains the skin orange for the duration of therapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. This is the practical warning that prevents a patient ruining clothes and abandoning the drug. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Why is epinephrine added to a local anaesthetic?","choices":["It speeds onset","It reduces allergy","It buffers the pH","It prolongs the block"],"answer":3,"exp":"Vasoconstriction at the injection site. — 03. ANS Pharmacology, Slide 85"},{"q":"Which allylamine is oral as well as topical?","choices":["Naftifine is oral and topical; terbinafine is topical only","Both are available only topically","Both are available only orally","Terbinafine is oral and topical; naftifine is topical only"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Both inhibit squalene epoxidase and are used for superficial dermatophyte infection. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"Which antiviral approach does the acyclovir family represent?","choices":["Inhibiting viral assembly","Inhibiting nucleic acid synthesis","Inhibiting viral release","Blocking uncoating after entry"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the drug is incorporated into viral DNA and terminates the chain. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 118"},{"q":"Why is renal function watched on acyclovir?","choices":["It causes interstitial nephritis as a hypersensitivity reaction","The drug crystallises in the renal tubule, so hydration must be maintained","The drug is nephrotoxic through direct tubular necrosis","Its vehicle accumulates in renal impairment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a physical rather than a toxic mechanism of renal injury. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"How is the efferent division of the peripheral nervous system divided?","choices":["Into the brain and the spinal cord, which together form the outflow","Somatic, under voluntary control, and autonomic, under involuntary control","Into the afferent and enteric systems","Into the sympathetic and parasympathetic systems only, with no somatic component"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Afferent carries signals to the central nervous system; efferent carries them away and splits this way. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which electrolyte and renal effects does amphotericin B cause?","choices":["Isolated hepatotoxicity with no renal involvement","Bone marrow suppression as the dose-limiting toxicity","Low potassium and magnesium, hypotension, renal tubule damage","High potassium and calcium with a rising filtration rate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The saline hydration is the practical countermeasure worth remembering. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"Where in the folate pathway does each half of Bactrim act?","choices":["Sulfa at the PABA step, trimethoprim at the reductase","Sulfa at the reductase, trimethoprim at the PABA step","Both inhibit dihydrofolate reductase, at different binding sites","Sulfa blocks gut folate; trimethoprim blocks reabsorption"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Two sequential steps in one pathway, which is what makes the combination synergistic. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"Which azole enters the brain and treats cryptococcal meningitis?","choices":["Caspofungin","Fluconazole","Ketoconazole","Itraconazole"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It also has the best oral absorption of the group and is excreted in the urine, which suits it to candidiasis. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"What are epinephrine's emergency indications?","choices":["Anaphylactic shock and cardiac arrest","Cardiogenic and septic shock","Hypertensive emergency","Chronic heart failure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside acute reversal of bronchospasm. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"What are ampicillin and amoxicillin drug of choice for?","choices":["Enterococcus, Listeria, endocarditis prophylaxis and pneumonia","Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus bacteraemia and osteomyelitis","Pseudomonal, nosocomial and polymicrobial infection","Syphilis, gas gangrene, and meningococcal disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Aminopenicillins add some Gram-negative aerobic activity to the natural penicillin spectrum but still lack Staphylococcus coverage. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What is the vancomycin loading dose in a critically ill patient?","choices":["15 to 20 milligrams per kilogram, rounded to the nearest 500 milligrams","7 milligrams per kilogram once daily","1 gram regardless of weight","25 to 30 milligrams per kilogram, rounded to the nearest 500 milligrams"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The loading dose exists to reach the target trough quickly rather than waiting through several maintenance doses. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Why is caution advised with dobutamine in atrial fibrillation?","choices":["It depletes cardiac catecholamine stores","It increases atrioventricular conduction","It slows sinoatrial firing","It causes bronchoconstriction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which can accelerate the ventricular response. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"Why is ketoconazole rarely used now?","choices":["No CNS entry, and it inhibits CYP3A4","Intravenous only, with severe infusion reactions","It is the only azole ineffective against Candida","It causes visual disturbance in about 30 percent of patients"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and itraconazole with its wider spectrum largely replaced it. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"From what is acetylcholine synthesised?","choices":["Glutamate and glycine","Tryptophan and serotonin","Acetyl coenzyme A and choline","Tyrosine and dopamine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and choline is recovered by recycling after the transmitter is degraded. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which problem is specific to topical neomycin?","choices":["It bleaches hair and clothing","It causes hypopigmentation","It is inactivated by benzoyl peroxide","It frequently causes sensitisation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the class can also accumulate systemically. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What is another term for an indirect-acting cholinergic agonist?","choices":["Anticholinesterase","Parasympatholytic","Antimuscarinic","Choline ester"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. They raise acetylcholine by blocking its degradation rather than binding the receptor. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What distinguishes a bacteriostatic drug from a bactericidal one?","choices":["Bacteriostatic agents are given orally and bactericidal agents are given intravenously","Static halts growth; cidal disrupts enough to kill","Static is Gram-positive only; cidal covers both","Static needs a higher concentration than cidal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The split is about whether the drug itself kills, not about how fast it works. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Pustular inflammation on the trunk weeks after starting a steroid. What happens when it stops?","choices":["No change, because the eruption is independent of the drug","An initial worsening, because inflammation increases","Immediate and complete resolution of the eruption","Conversion of the pustules into open comedones"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and warning the patient of this in advance is what stops them abandoning the plan. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What kind of agent is dopamine?","choices":["Multiple functions by location, and usually inhibitory","Purely excitatory, controlling muscle movement in spinal cord neurons","The major inhibitory transmitter of the brain","Involved chiefly in mood, appetite and sensory perception"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which contrasts with acetylcholine being usually excitatory. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A patient complains their pillowcases are going pale. Which agent?","choices":["Benzoyl peroxide","Tretinoin","Clindamycin lotion","Azelaic acid"],"answer":0,"exp":"It bleaches hair, clothes and fabric. — 02. Dermatology Medications, Slide 21"},{"q":"Why does a local anaesthetic solution often contain epinephrine?","choices":["It provides additional analgesia of its own","It prevents an allergic reaction to the anaesthetic","It causes vasoconstriction at the injection site","It speeds systemic absorption of the anaesthetic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and applied topically it also helps control capillary oozing. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"What is the dual mechanism of action of the fluoroquinolones?","choices":["Inhibits DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV","Inhibition of the 30S and 50S ribosomal subunits simultaneously","Sulfa at the reductase, trimethoprim upstream of it","Inhibition of cell wall cross-linking and of beta-lactamase"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The topoisomerase IV mechanism is poorly understood. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A patient on clarithromycin has a rising digoxin level. What is the mechanism?","choices":["Renal competition","Protein displacement","CYP3A4 inhibition","Cation chelation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Macrolide metabolites form an inactive complex with CYP3A. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals, Slide 59"},{"q":"Which beta blocker is used topically for chronic open-angle glaucoma?","choices":["Atenolol","Carvedilol","Timolol","Propranolol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Reduces aqueous humour production. — 03. ANS Pharmacology, Slide 119"},{"q":"What is atropine's antisecretory use?","choices":["Reducing gastric acid secretion in peptic ulcer disease","Reducing bronchial secretions in asthma maintenance","Preventing sweating in axillary hyperhidrosis","Blocking respiratory tract secretions before surgery"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and glycopyrrolate is used similarly before surgery. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What does norepinephrine act as?","choices":["A hormone only, released from the adrenal cortex","An inhibitory transmitter in pain pathways of the spinal cord","Both a neurotransmitter and a hormone","A neurotransmitter only, with no hormonal role"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it also regulates normal brain processes, usually excitatory. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A patient in acute heart failure needs inotropic support. Which agent?","choices":["Propranolol","Dobutamine","Phenylephrine","Clonidine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Raises cardiac output without much rise in oxygen demand. — 03. ANS Pharmacology, Slide 94"},{"q":"Which poisoning does physostigmine treat?","choices":["Overdose of a cholinesterase inhibitor insecticide","Beta blocker overdose","Opioid overdose","Overdose of anticholinergic drugs such as atropine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It raises acetylcholine to compete with the blocking drug. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What is mupirocin's mechanism?","choices":["It binds bacterial transfer RNA, preventing protein synthesis","It prevents bacterial cell wall synthesis","It interrupts the bacterial cytoplasmic membrane","It inhibits fungal ergosterol production and so prevents cell wall synthesis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it is not absorbed, though it may irritate mucous membranes. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What is the defining clinical advantage of aztreonam?","choices":["The broadest beta-lactam spectrum, including anaerobes","Oral bioavailability allowing outpatient completion of therapy","Reliable central nervous system penetration for meningitis","No beta-lactam cross-reactivity; safe in true allergy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That property is what makes a monobactam worth having despite its narrow Gram-negative-only spectrum. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which is a LOCAL adverse effect of topical steroids, not a systemic one?","choices":["Cataracts","Growth retardation in children","Skin atrophy","Adrenal suppression"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Local effects are skin atrophy, acne, rosacea and allergic dermatitis related to the vehicle. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which azole has the best oral absorption?","choices":["Fluconazole, excreted in the urine","Ketoconazole, excreted in the bile","Itraconazole, excreted unchanged in the faeces","Posaconazole, excreted by the lungs"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and its urinary excretion is part of why a single dose treats vaginal candidiasis. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"Which two problems follow intralesional steroid injection of a nodule?","choices":["Systemic absorption leading to adrenal suppression, and local tissue atrophy","Permanent hyperpigmentation and keloid formation","Bleaching of hair and clothing","Photosensitivity and severe sunburn"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both are consequences of injecting a steroid into the lesion. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Why is tacrolimus ointment second line rather than first for atopic dermatitis?","choices":["Possible cancer risk","It is ineffective","It causes atrophy","It cannot be used on the face"],"answer":0,"exp":"Second line after topical steroids. — 02. Dermatology Medications, Slide 43"},{"q":"What distinguishes dobutamine from other sympathomimetics?","choices":["It produces no tolerance with prolonged use","It barely raises myocardial oxygen demand","It has no effect on atrioventricular conduction","It can be given orally"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which matters in a failing heart. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"What does acyclovir achieve if started within 24 hours of chickenpox?","choices":["It cures the infection and prevents later reactivation as shingles","It has no effect unless combined with valacyclovir","It prevents transmission to contacts but does not alter the illness","It shortens the acute illness but does not cure the infection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the virus is not eradicated, which is why reactivation as shingles remains possible later. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"From what source is amphotericin B derived?","choices":["Penicillium griseofulvum","Bacillus polymyxa","The soil bacterium Amycolatopsis orientalis","A soil Streptomycetaceae organism"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which places it alongside several antibacterials in being a natural product of a soil microbe. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"Most topical acne agents irritate. What does that cause, and how should you start?","choices":["It is prevented entirely by using an alcoholic solution","Patients stop; start low and increase gradually","It confirms the drug is working, so maintain the starting strength","Switch straight to systemic therapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Anticipating the irritation is what keeps a patient on therapy. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which two antivirals are given intravenously as the parent compound?","choices":["Valacyclovir and famciclovir, each with an intravenous prodrug","Oseltamivir and acyclovir, neither having an oral form","Valganciclovir and valacyclovir, given intravenously in transplant patients","Penciclovir and ganciclovir, each with an oral prodrug available"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — famciclovir and valganciclovir respectively are the better-absorbed oral forms. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 125"},{"q":"Which fluoroquinolone should not be used for a urinary tract infection?","choices":["All are suitable","Moxifloxacin","Ciprofloxacin","Levofloxacin"],"answer":1,"exp":"The one that does not cover urinary tract infection. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals, Slide 78"},{"q":"What is the activity profile of cefazolin and cephalexin?","choices":["Activity restricted to atypical organisms such as Legionella and Mycoplasma","Gram-positives without Enterococcus, some Gram-negative cover","Gram-negatives including Pseudomonas, plus Enterococcus","Broad coverage including anaerobes and methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The absence of Enterococcus coverage is a recurring theme across the whole cephalosporin class. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What is phenylephrine used for?","choices":["Hypertension and opiate withdrawal","Glaucoma","Nasal decongestant, eye redness and septic shock","Asthma and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"How should griseofulvin be taken with food?","choices":["Food has no effect on absorption","It should be taken with an antacid to reduce gastric irritation","Absorption is increased by a high-fat meal, because the drug is very lipid soluble","It must be taken on an empty stomach, since food chelates and inactivates the drug"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it is effective against numerous dermatophytes but not Candida, acting fungistatically. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"Why is influenza treatment framed around a season?","choices":["Resistance disappears outside the season","Renal dosing requirements change with the season","Flu season runs roughly October to March, which is when the illness is circulating","The drug is only manufactured and distributed during those particular months"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it frames when an influenza-like illness should raise suspicion in the first place. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 126"},{"q":"A patient on warfarin is started on Bactrim. What happens to the INR?","choices":["It becomes unmeasurable","It rises significantly","It falls","It is unchanged"],"answer":1,"exp":"Through CYP2C9 inhibition. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals, Slide 85"},{"q":"When should a vancomycin trough be drawn?","choices":["Immediately before the second dose","At any point within the dosing interval, provided the exact time of the draw is recorded","15 to 30 minutes before the next scheduled dose, usually around the third or fourth dose","30 minutes after the end of the infusion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Drawing around the third or fourth dose is what makes the level reflect steady state. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What route and dose adjustment does oseltamivir need?","choices":["Available intravenously only, with hepatic adjustment","Available orally and intravenously, with no adjustment needed","Available as an inhaled powder only","Available orally only, and requiring renal dose adjustment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Renal adjustment is a consideration rather than a dose to memorise. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals.pptx, Slide 127"},{"q":"What does imiquimod do?","choices":["It is converted to benzoic acid after crossing the stratum corneum","Corrects keratinization, cuts bacteria and inflammation","Drives interferon alpha and tumour necrosis factor release","It inhibits T cell, mast cell and keratinocyte activation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is an immunomodulator rather than a directly cytotoxic agent. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which adverse effect is specific to atracurium?","choices":["Rebound tachycardia on withdrawal","Histamine release, with a fall in blood pressure and flushing","Malignant hyperthermia in susceptible people","Prolonged paralysis from pseudocholinesterase deficiency"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it was replaced by its isomer cisatracurium for having fewer adverse effects. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which retinoid is used for Kaposi sarcoma?","choices":["Alitretinoin","Bexarotene","Tazarotene","Adapalene"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bexarotene is the one paired with T-cell lymphoma. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which azole penetrates the central nervous system for cryptococcal meningitis?","choices":["Itraconazole","Griseofulvin","Fluconazole","Ketoconazole"],"answer":2,"exp":"Best oral absorption and it penetrates the CNS. — Antibiotics, Antivirals, and Antifungals, Slide 106"},{"q":"Why is tyramine dangerous in a patient taking a monoamine oxidase inhibitor?","choices":["It blocks reuptake of norepinephrine at the terminal","It is converted into amphetamine in the liver","It directly stimulates alpha-one receptors","Monoamine oxidase normally destroys it in the gut"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it is found in fermented foods such as cheese and wine. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"What happens if oxymetazoline is used beyond three days?","choices":["Nasal septal perforation","Rhinitis medicamentosa, or rebound congestion","Permanent loss of the sense of smell","Systemic hypertension requiring treatment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it is the practical reason for a hard stop on a decongestant spray. — 03. ANS Pharmacology(1).pptx, Slide 95"}]};
